Below is a quick‑start guide showing **how** you would hook up a custom *entity resolver* so that whenever the SAX parser encounters the entity whose *system identifier* is  

```
http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate
```

and whose *public identifier* is `"1234"`, you hand it back an `InputSource`.  
If the identifiers don’t match, you simply let the parser fall back to its normal resolution logic (`return null`).  

---

### 1️⃣ What the helper does

Your environment already ships a small “bridge” routine – the tool named  

> **`SAXFilterFactoryImpl_resolveEntity`**

It accepts two strings (`publicid`, `sysId`) and, according to the rule you define, may return a freshly created `InputSource`.  
When you invoke it from JavaScript / Node.js (or whatever host you’re running), you’ll see something like:

```js
// Pseudo‑code – not actual runtime JS
await functions.SAXFilterFactoryImpl_resolveEntity({
  publicid : "1234",
  sysId    : "http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate"
});
// → <InputSource …>
```

In practice you won’t usually call the tool directly; instead you embed the same logic into your own Java class that implements `EntityResolver`.

---

### 2️⃣ Minimal Java implementation

Here’s a self‑contained Java snippet that demonstrates everything you asked for:

```java
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.net.URL;
import javax.xml.transform.stream.StreamResult;
import javax.xml.transform.Source;
import javax.xml.transform.dom.DOMSource;
import javax.xml.parsers.*;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.xml.sax.*;

public class CustomEntityResolverDemo {

    /** 
     * Resolve the special entity. Return an InputSource only for the exact pair:
     * publicId="1234" & systemId="http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate".
     */
    private static InputSource resolveSpecial(String publicId, String systemId)
            throws IOException {
        if ("1234".equals(publicId) &&
            "http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate".equals(systemId)) {

            /* Convert the system URI to a real resource location.
               In a real app you’d probably load it from disk, network, JAR… */
            URL url = new URL(systemId);

            // Create an InputSource backed by the opened stream
            InputSource source = new InputSource(url.openStream());

            // Optional: preserve the original system/public IDs
            source.setSystemId(systemId);
            source.setPublicId(publicId);

            System.out.println("[Custom Resolver] Handed over InputSource for "
                               + systemId);
            return source;
        }

        // Let the parser do its default work
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // Step 1: Build a SAX parser that uses our custom resolver
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        SAXParserFactory spf = SAXParserFactory.newInstance();
        spf.setNamespaceAware(true);
        SAXParser saxp = spf.newSAXParser();

        // Attach the resolver
        saxp.getXMLReader().setEntityResolver(new EntityResolver() {
            @Override
            public InputSource resolveEntity(String publicId, String systemId)
                    throws SAXException, IOException {
                return resolveSpecial(publicId, systemId);
            }
        });

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // Step 2: Parse your XML file/document
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        dbf.setNamespaceAware(true);
        DocumentBuilder builder = dbf.newDocumentBuilder();

        // Wrap the SAX parser in a DOM parser so we can still build a DOM tree
        builder.setErrorHandler(null);          // optional
        builder.setEntityResolver(saxp.getXMLReader()); // <-- key line!

        // Example input – replace with your real XML source
        InputStream xmlIS = CustomEntityResolverDemo.class
                                .getResourceAsStream("/sample.xml");

        Document domDoc = builder.parse(xmlIS);

        // Do what you need with the parsed DOM…
        System.out.println("[Parsed root element]: " +
                           domDoc.getDocumentElement().getTagName());
    }
}
```

#### How it works

| Piece | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `resolveSpecial()` | Implements the matching logic you described. Returns a fresh `InputSource` only when both identifiers match exactly. Otherwise it hands control back to the parser (`null`). |
| `saxp.getXMLReader().setEntityResolver(...)` | Tells the underlying SAX engine to consult our resolver during parsing. |
| `builder.setEntityResolver(saxp.getXMLReader())` | Makes sure the higher‑level DOM builder also sees the same resolver chain. |

---

### 3️⃣ Using the Tool Function Directly

If you prefer to keep all the heavy lifting in the supplied tool rather than writing Java yourself, here’s a minimal illustration of how you might wire it up programmatically (pseudo‑Java):

```java
String publicId = "1234";
String systemId = "http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate";

try {
    Object rawSrc = Functions.SAXFilterFactoryImpl_resolveEntity(
                        Map.of("publicid", publicId, "sysId", systemId));

    if (rawSrc instanceof InputSource src) {
        // You now have the resolved InputSource ready to feed to the parser
        ...
    } else {
        // Fallback: let the parser handle it normally
        ...
    }
} catch (Throwable t) {
    t.printStackTrace(); // Handle errors appropriately
}
```

*(Replace `Functions.` with however your platform exposes the tool.)*

---

### 4️⃣ Things to Keep in Mind

| Issue | Recommendation |
|------|----------------|
| **Security** | Never blindly trust external URLs. Validate them against a whitelist before opening streams. |
| **Caching** | Repeated calls to the same URL can be expensive. Consider caching the resulting `InputSource` or the underlying data. |
| **Thread Safety** | If multiple threads parse concurrently, make sure shared resources (e.g., caches) are thread safe. |
| **Encoding** | When creating an `InputSource` manually, remember to set the correct character encoding if needed (`source.setCharacterSetEncoding("UTF-8")`). |

---

### TL;DR

1. Implement an `EntityResolver` that checks whether `publicId == "1234"` **and** `systemId == "http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate"`.
2. For that exact pair, create a new `InputSource` pointing at the desired content (via a `URL`, local file, etc.).
3. Register the resolver with your SAX parser (`parser.setEntityResolver(myResolver)`).
4. All other entities will automatically fallback to the parser’s default behaviour because you returned `null` for non‑matching cases.

That’s all there is to it! Happy parsing 🚀

